    n "Sai's watch. More specifically, the date \"9999-12-31\"."
    n "When I found Sai, he should've been in the attic. This means that it's very possible he traveled through time in some capacity, even though he kept denying it. If he did, his watch would've done the same."
    n "\"9999-12-31\" hints at that even further.{w} It's the highest date the watch could display, so it could be linked to another common concept related to time travel: loops."
    n "Looping through time—reliving the same portion of time over and over. I'm jumping to this thought not only because of the date, but also because the battery ran out before the time it was supposed to."
    nvl clear
    with dissolve
    n "For example, if the watch is in the process of traveling from {time=12:20} to the past, until it reaches the travel point, the time displayed would increase per travel. If that's repeated infinite times, the date would eventually hit its maximum value and stay there."
    n "If the watch had repeated that process infinitely when I saw it, the date could make a bit of sense. Similarly, every few years, the battery would run out, so replacing it would allow the time to continue advancing."
    n "So, assuming something of the sort is what happened, is it a coincidence that I just so happened to experience the loop in which the battery had to be replaced?{w} There would be times in which the watch would appear functional and ones in which the date wouldn't have reached the year 9,999 yet. All of those would have to reach the same loop point, which contradicts the way I thought this worked, because traveling to the past would end up modifying it in different manners."
    n "But I suppose it's pointless to think about this because a bunch of other contradictions prevent anything like that from ever happening."
    nvl clear
    with dissolve
    n "To begin with, if the wristwatch had traveled back insane amounts of times, it would have physically degraded. If it had survived at least the 8,000 years needed to reach 9,999, the metal would've appeared rusty, and it's hard to believe it wouldn't have just disintegrated instantly."
    n "Also, Sai had to be the one to bring it back through time, so he'd be older, and he couldn't have existed for the same number of years without dying. This would be circumvented if going to the past replicated your past body, but then the watch's date and time would reset after every travel too."
    n "This is not how the Sekimeiya works. If you travel thirty minutes to the past, during those thirty minutes, two versions of you exist at the same time. If Sai went back with his watch that many times, he would have created a new copy of himself each travel."
    n "That isn't all. Naturally, I'm thinking about this to justify the watch's date, but there's no apparent reason why Sai would travel back to the same spot more than once.{w} And if he somehow could circumvent aging, it'd mean that after the point he traveled from, he couldn't exist anymore; if there is a point in time in which he always travels back, he could never make it past that point. Despite that, I'm sure that if I go to the attic right now, I'll see him."
