    n "You want to cross a hallway full of doors and deadly traps, but you can think of many ways to do it and you're not sure which one will work."
    n "With the Sekimeiya, if you could be the creator of causal loops, assuring success in these situations would be a way to use them."
    n "You could think: \"I will try doors 1, 3, and 5, and I'll walk touching the right wall. After I make it across, I will go around from the outside and leave a mark behind the door to this place.\""
    n "If you look where you'll leave a mark and find it, you'll know that arrangement of doors and movements will work. If the mark isn't there, you'll know you would die midway, rendering you unable to leave the mark.{w} You can keep thinking of different arrangements until the mark appears. Since you'll only move forth once you see the mark, it's an effective bootstrap—the end result is what allows it to happen. But despite being a \"paradox\", there's actually an origin to it."
    nvl clear
